WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.430
FELIPE HOFFA: Analyzing
data is like cooking.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.130
You pick your data sets, your
ingredients, you clean them,

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.400
you slice them.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.170
And then you serve your results.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.330
I'm Felipe Hoffa,
and in this video,

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:12.690
I'm going to travel
to Dresden, Germany,

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.370
to meet the team
fighting spam at LOVOO.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.810
LOVOO is an online dating
app, and their engineers

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.250
use data to identify
and block spammers

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.580
trying to abuse their system.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.440
In this episode,
we're going to learn

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.710
how they define features to
train a logistic regression

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.840
model to find who's a spammer.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.720
And now they can do all
of these inside the query.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.730
And because we love
food, we are going

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.002
to do all this while cooking.

00:00:38.002 --> 00:00:40.212
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.270
Here we have Juan
de Dios Santos,

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.520
one of the Big Data engineers
fighting spam for LOVOO.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.020
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
It's likely so.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:47.620
Hi, everyone.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.662
My name is Juan, and I'm
one of the anti-spammers

00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:50.630
here at LOVOO.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:54.180
And my role here is to design
and to develop new techniques

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.360
and algorithms to detect the
spammer from our platform

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.130
and to avoid the
proliferation of them.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.890
So today, since we're going
to be talking about spam,

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.810
I thought that the
most ideal thing would

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.700
be to cook something with spam.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.620
So I'm going to be
preparing one typical dish

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.300
from my country,
Puerto Rico, which

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.743
is called spam guisada,
or just simply jamonilla.

00:01:12.743 --> 00:01:14.660
FELIPE HOFFA: Ooh, I'm
getting hungry already.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.480
[SALSA MUSIC]

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.500
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
It's really important

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.338
to know what exactly spammer
and normal users are doing,

00:01:24.338 --> 00:01:26.880
because of course they are going
to behave in different ways.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.410
For example, spammers, they
do normally the same action

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:31.790
quite often.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.435
And normal users-- well,
they just ask normal.

00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:36.560
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, how do
you decide what's normal

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.610
and what's not normal?

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.240
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
So the tracer

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.370
has three different
kind of actions.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.530
We have what is called the
active action, which are

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.430
the things the user is doing.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.880
For example, I like you,
I send you one message.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:48.810
And then we also have
the passive actions,

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.140
which are the things that
you are doing up on me.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.850
You are sending me one
message, you are liking me.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.780
And then we have the time, which
is how much time it has been

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.456
between each different action.

00:01:59.456 --> 00:02:02.444
[SALSA MUSIC]

00:02:03.822 --> 00:02:06.280
FELIPE HOFFA: You have your
whole system processing images,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.690
processing events.

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.090
And you're also using BigQuery.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.840
I would love to know
how you use BigQuery.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.923
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: So
BigQuery-- we use BigQuery

00:02:12.923 --> 00:02:14.530
mostly for sorting our data.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.865
You know, for storing our
historical data, prediction

00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:19.640
data, training set and so on.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.040
However, recently we
start to use BigQuery

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.360
to do machine learning on it.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.985
[SILENCE]

00:02:25.985 --> 00:02:28.110
FELIPE HOFFA: Can you show
me what are your tables?

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.420
How do you do machine
learning inside BigQuery?

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.420
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: All
right, let's get on it.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.800
So first, here, we
have our data set.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.110
And inside our data set,
we have one small group

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.350
of tables, which are
called the sample tables.

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:40.990
And in these sample
tables, I'm going

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.860
to keep just that-- just
small sample tiny versions

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.570
of our big data set.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.270
So here, let's take a look
at what I call the Sheriff.

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.472
Sherrif is one of our
machine learning models.

00:02:50.472 --> 00:02:52.680
And this one is going to
predict if you are a spammer

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.370
or not based on the
things that you are doing

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.110
and some of the characteristics
of your profiles.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.700
And here we have two date sets.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.932
We have the training
one and the testing one.

00:03:01.932 --> 00:03:04.140
The training one, of course,
for training the system,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.138
and the testing one
is for testing it.

00:03:06.138 --> 00:03:07.930
FELIPE HOFFA: And in
the training data set,

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.480
you have all of your columns.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.480
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Yeah,
and here, each column

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.070
represent our features.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.120
And, unfortunately, I
need to hide some of them.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.680
So that's why you
only see numbers.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.620
But, however, we can focus
on the one called message

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.110
sent, like, and likes created.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.000
And this is saying just that--

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.810
these are the messages
that I sent to you,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.140
these are the likes that
I have done to others,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.805
and the people
that have liked me.

00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:32.300
FELIPE HOFFA: OK, so this
table has, like, 200,000 rows.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.008
What is each row?

00:03:33.008 --> 00:03:34.550
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
Yeah, like here,

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.720
each row represent one
observation, so one user.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:37.500
FELIPE HOFFA: One user.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:37.930
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Yeah.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.430
FELIPE HOFFA: OK, so, for each
user, just summarize features

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:40.930
that--

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.470
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: It's
like-- so here-- so like I say,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.540
these data sets, this
training is actually

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.140
made of two different things.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.910
The first one is,
what are you doing?

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.140
But we calculate this by looking
at the ratio of execution

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.380
of events.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.240
Meaning that, of all the
events that you have done,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.870
what is the percentage
of message sent?

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.540
And in this case, we have
one user, our user number 5,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.360
and we can see that
the values are on 39%.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.970
Meaning that of all the
event the person have done,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:12.138
39% of them has
been message sent.

00:04:12.138 --> 00:04:12.930
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.500
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Got it?

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:14.360
FELIPE HOFFA: I got it.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:15.180
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Perfect.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.347
FELIPE HOFFA: And you
determined this feature seem--

00:04:17.347 --> 00:04:18.360
like experimenting?

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:18.820
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
It's like it.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.089
I mean, you try with
this, you try with that.

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:22.580
You lose some and you gain some.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.780
You know, just a matter
of experimentation.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.140
[SALSA MUSIC]

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:35.570
FELIPE HOFFA: How
do you train this?

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:36.487
How do you determine--

00:04:36.487 --> 00:04:38.195
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
How do I train this.

00:04:38.195 --> 00:04:40.090
Well, we have our
features-- or, I

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.770
mean, the BigQuery
feature of how

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.830
to do machine learning
inside BigQuery.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.010
And first, we need to go to
our query editor, of course.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.910
And then we are going
to execute our query.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.710
And here we have this
kind of simple query,

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.658
and it's called
Create or Replace.

00:04:57.658 --> 00:04:59.200
And this is going
to do exactly that.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.230
It's going to create
one model, or in case

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:05.578
the model already exists,
it's going to replace it.

00:05:05.578 --> 00:05:06.370
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:07.995
What are your options here?

00:05:07.995 --> 00:05:09.370
JUAN DE DIOS
SANTOS: My options--

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.620
so the first one, the
most important one,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:15.040
is our model type, which is what
kind of model I want to train.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.103
In this case, I want to
use logistic regression.

00:05:17.103 --> 00:05:18.520
FELIPE HOFFA:
Logistic regression,

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:19.780
that allows you to--

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.830
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: To
do binary classification.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.965
Either 0 or 1, true or
false, spammer or hammer.

00:05:24.965 --> 00:05:26.590
FELIPE HOFFA: I see,
spammer or hammer.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:27.798
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Hammer--

00:05:27.798 --> 00:05:29.980
oh, yeah, that's how we call it.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.110
FELIPE HOFFA: And, with your--

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.600
that's where you
say, I want to get--

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.010
to predict this columns, spam.

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:36.940
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
It's likely the other.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.080
The second one is
input label columns.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.370
And here we specify which is
the column that is our target--

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.085
in this case, going to be spam.

00:05:44.085 --> 00:05:46.210
FELIPE HOFFA: And we also
have some options there--

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:46.500
additional--

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.708
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Exactly.

00:05:47.708 --> 00:05:49.150
So I also have max iteration.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.640
And this one is set to 50.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.450
And this means that
my system is going

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.330
to train for 50 iterations.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:58.870
Normally, in this case, it's
no long-- that's not necessary.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.410
Because I'm really truly kind
of [INAUDIBLE] on five or six.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:05.830
However, for the next
part, I want to show you

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.680
how the loss function decrease.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.620
And for that, I'm
setting us to 50

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.240
so we can have more data so
we can see a prettier curve.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.010
Also, for that, we
need to set early stop

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.310
to false-- really important.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.130
Otherwise, it's just
going to stop early.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.971
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool, so let's
start training this model.

00:06:20.971 --> 00:06:24.740
[SALSA MUSIC]

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.000
We are getting some results.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.583
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
At least, so far,

00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:29.110
since we used max
iteration of 50.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:31.640
We are currently on
iteration number 41.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.370
As you can see, it's taking
around 6 to 7 seconds

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:36.830
to actually-- for
each iteration.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.290
So it's quite fast.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.950
However, we can realize here
that the training loss is not

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:41.950
changing much.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.940
However, like I said before,
I was expecting this.

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.620
I just want to have
50 iterations just

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.493
to see how the loss
function looks like.

00:06:48.493 --> 00:06:50.160
Because I just like
to visualize things.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.260
FELIPE HOFFA: And how
would you visualize this?

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.510
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Well,
let's do it in Data Studio.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.300
One of my favorite
features, let's say,

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:59.490
of BigQuery is that we have
this cool button here--

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.030
really powerful button--

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.120
which is called
Explore in Data Studio.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.760
And when you click there, this
is going to kind of migrate--

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.280
to take your whole data set.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.860
And it's going to move it
to a new Data Studio window.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:14.328
And here, in Data Studio, then
we can visualize our data.

00:07:14.328 --> 00:07:15.120
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.080
So you can just now
select from wherever

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.432
the training info [INAUDIBLE]?

00:07:20.432 --> 00:07:21.640
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Exactly.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:22.770
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.780
FELIPE HOFFA: And then
we can get this data out

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.552
into Data Studio.

00:07:25.552 --> 00:07:26.760
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Exactly.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.052
And here, this is how it looks.

00:07:28.052 --> 00:07:30.880
Let me first-- we need
to move a lot of buttons

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.550
here and drag and drop.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.470
But essentially,
this is how our--

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.652
FELIPE HOFFA: On the
y-axis, we have the loss?

00:07:36.652 --> 00:07:38.610
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
Exactly, we have the loss.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.890
And then on the x-axis we
have the number of iteration.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:45.540
And here, like expected, once
again in around iteration five

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.490
or four, the values
was already kind

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.983
of getting to start more stable.

00:07:48.983 --> 00:07:50.400
FELIPE HOFFA:
Pretty low, and then

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.498
you get to a minimum of 0.0--

00:07:52.498 --> 00:07:54.790
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Yes,
like it's really, really low.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:56.630
It's between 0.1 and 0.2.

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:58.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.623
Maybe like 0.15, 0.14.

00:07:59.623 --> 00:08:01.665
FELIPE HOFFA: That's pretty
good and pretty fast.

00:08:01.665 --> 00:08:03.750
Oh, oh, oh, by the
way-- now you get

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.540
these visualizations
automatically inside the query

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.045
once the training is done.

00:08:08.045 --> 00:08:10.230
And we can also look at
the stats of this model.

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:10.930
Or where the
training statistics--

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:12.580
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Yeah,
this is actually pretty cool.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.913
So you know that, to make sure--

00:08:13.913 --> 00:08:16.500
I mean, if we want to know
if the model is either good

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.780
or not, then we need
to have some metrics.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.020
And here, we have all
the five or six metrics,

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:22.890
just out of the box.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.840
We don't have to
calculate anything.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.900
And for example, we can see
precision, recall accuracy,

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.027
F1 score, the log function--

00:08:30.027 --> 00:08:31.860
I mean, the loss function
that we just saw--

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:33.563
and the area under the curve.

00:08:33.563 --> 00:08:34.980
And by just looking
at this value,

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.870
I will say that
it's pretty good.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.055
We have an accuracy
of almost 96%,

00:08:39.055 --> 00:08:42.760
while our F1 score is almost 92
and the area under the curve--

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.370
I want to say it's
really excellent.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:46.370
FELIPE HOFFA: I like it.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.710
And once you have your
whole model trained,

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.380
what's behind the model.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.255
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
What's behind the model.

00:08:53.255 --> 00:08:55.570
So in this case, since we're
using logistic regression,

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.350
basically we have a bunch of
things we call the weights,

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.790
or the coefficients.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:02.380
Which, if I explain
quick and easy,

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:05.850
logistic regression is basically
one huge mathematical equation

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.930
with a lot of different values.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.780
And these values are
actually what we're

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.030
going to learn during training.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.430
So at the end of the day,
all these fancy thing

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.750
just transforms into
a couple of numbers--

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.570
our coefficients
and the intercept.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.362
FELIPE HOFFA: And with
this equal function,

00:09:20.362 --> 00:09:21.487
we can get our weights out.

00:09:21.487 --> 00:09:23.570
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
Actually, good question, yes.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.800
If we would like to
implement this in production

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.600
into another system,
then we just only

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.750
need to export all
of these values.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:33.330
And then we should build
the equation ourself.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.890
And then just by
plugging in the values.

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.298
And then we have our model.

00:09:37.298 --> 00:09:39.090
FELIPE HOFFA: Well, so
this is really great

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.580
because, basically, you gave
a query-- this is my table,

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:42.470
these are my labels.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:43.678
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Exactly.

00:09:43.678 --> 00:09:46.140
FELIPE HOFFA: And just
ran a logistic regression.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:47.610
JUAN DE DIOS
SANTOS: Yeah, right.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.550
FELIPE HOFFA: And you got a lot
of these just automatically.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:54.060
And that's one way in which
LOVOO identifies spam--

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.860
online dating spam.

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:57.660
Now, with BigQuery,
it's really easy

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.290
to start iterating the features
and the training of your model

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.460
without leaving
the data warehouse.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:06.030
But dinner is not ready
until we serve it.

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.190
How do we know how
well the anti-spam team

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:09.450
is doing at LOVOO?

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.890
Let's go back to the kitchen.

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:12.090
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
Serving here in this case

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.440
will be, of course, to serve
the model in production.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:16.618
However, more than that,
I need to make sure

00:10:16.618 --> 00:10:17.785
how the model is performing.

00:10:17.785 --> 00:10:20.285
You know, I want to know
how our results are.

00:10:20.285 --> 00:10:21.202
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:10:21.202 --> 00:10:22.870
And how are you doing so far?

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.662
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
How are we doing this.

00:10:24.662 --> 00:10:25.950
So we have some numbers here.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.670
These are from our
transparency reports.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.680
And, basically, this is
some report that we do,

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:32.490
where we publish--

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:35.430
we make public all
these values so people

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:39.240
can have an idea of how
our system is performing.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.520
And in the third
quarter of 2016,

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.820
our percentage of
spammers was only 0.3%.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:48.712
Meaning that, of all our users,
we only have 0.3% of them

00:10:48.712 --> 00:10:49.670
were actually spammers.

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.338
It's less than 1%.

00:10:51.338 --> 00:10:52.630
And the number is quite stable.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:55.440
As we can see here, it's
like 0.3, 0.2, and so on.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.690
FELIPE HOFFA: And I guess
the number being stable

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:58.970
is a huge accomplishment.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.012
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: It's
good because it means--

00:11:01.012 --> 00:11:02.883
it's never going to
drop to 0, of course.

00:11:02.883 --> 00:11:04.800
But it's actually, if
the number keeps stable,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.300
if the number doesn't grow, it
means we're doing a good job.

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:08.662
Or so I think.

00:11:08.662 --> 00:11:10.620
FELIPE HOFFA: I guess
the spammers keep finding

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:12.110
new ways to exploit the system?

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:12.870
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
Yes, it's actually--

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.200
it's an eternal fight.

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:15.660
Like if I sleep--

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:19.095
if I get sloppy or anything,
then they will just go ahead.

00:11:19.095 --> 00:11:21.775
FELIPE HOFFA: But yes, you
are keeping them on the edge.

00:11:21.775 --> 00:11:23.400
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
And another metric

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.550
that I really like
to see is the one

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.400
that says the time it
takes to punish someone.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.920
Because, of course,
we are real time

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.020
and we want to get
the person now.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.040
So at the beginning, again
in the third quarter of 2016,

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.170
we had around 2.2 hours.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.130
After that, the
number went down.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:44.370
And then after that,
we had a huge drop

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:48.630
where it went from 2.1 to 1.1.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.150
It was like an hour less.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.340
And then suddenly,
something that happens-- you

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:55.630
know, like new
spammer ways or so on.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:56.880
And then the number increases.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.110
And then after that, it's
going down, down again.

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:01.360
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, that's
a huge drop at the end of--

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:01.920
JUAN DE DIOS
SANTOS: It is, yeah,

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:02.370
FELIPE HOFFA: --how
long it takes.

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:04.313
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS:
I'm really proud of it.

00:12:04.313 --> 00:12:05.730
FELIPE HOFFA: Well
done with that.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:06.210
JUAN DE DIOS SANTOS: Thank you.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.543
FELIPE HOFFA: So, as we serve
